  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nStrong high pressure will slide off the coast tonight, but still\nshould keep things mostly clear and calm. The remnants of \nHurricane Isaias lift into the OH Valley on Sunday with moisture\nfunneling up into southern areas late Sunday night into Monday \nmorning. \n\nStrong radiational cooling is forecast tonight. The Downeast\nzones will see plenty of frost and the advisories look good with\nno changes anticipated.\n\nBreezy southwest winds will allow some warmer air to funnel \ninto the area Sunday into Monday. Highs will top out in the 60s \nboth days, some 3-8 degrees above normal. \n\nAs the system moves off the southern New England coast, it \ncould bring a chance of showers for the Downeast coast late Sunday\nnight into Monday. However a new, strong high pressure will be \nbuilding across Quebec and will try and suppress rain chances.\nAt this time ensembles indicate a high PoP/low QPF threat for\njust the Downeast coastline and otherwise dry for the forecast\narea. Then as a frontal boundary sags into the area ahead of\nthat high pressure showers may develop and provide a better\nchance at rain showers.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA separate low pressure develops over the Great Lakes Monday \nnight as a trough deepens behind the departing tropical \nremnants. This will bring a much better chance of rain early-\nmid next week. Confidence remains low, but various models have \nalso some chilly 925 mb temps across the forecast area. This \nwill be especially true as the bulk of the forcing pushes into \nthe central highlands and North Woods. There could be enough \ncold air and latent cooling from melting to lead to light snow \nand some accumulations. The chance for snow at the onset of this\nsystem will depend greatly on the start time of precip, as if\nprecip does not start until mid to late morning, temperatures\nwill warm too much for any snowflakes to make it to the surface.\nTherefore the greatest chance for any snowfall will be deep\nwithin the North Woods through the early morning hours on\nTuesday.",
